    "I have not implemented anything yet. Which TDD level should I use: `none`, `targeted`, or `strict`? This workflow does not allow implementation until a level is chosen, and neither the repository nor your instructions set one. I recommend **`targeted`**: write the `--limit` tests first and confirm they fail, then change `todo.py`.\n\n**Result:** \u2753 Needs input. The plan below is ready to run once you pick a level.\n\n**Plan**\n- **Code:** In `todo.py:30`, add `--limit N` to the `list` subparser. `main()` prints only the first N tasks. The numbering stays 1..N. Without `--limit`, `list` prints every task, as it does now.\n- **Edge cases** (my defaults, change them if you want):\n  - If N is larger than the number of tasks, all tasks are printed.\n  - `--limit 0` prints nothing.\n  - A negative or non-integer N is rejected with an argparse usage error (exit code 2).\n- **Tests** in `tests/test_todo.py`, using the existing `main(argv, path, out)` setup:\n  1. With 3 tasks, `list --limit 2` prints `1. \u2026\\n2. \u2026\\n`.\n  2. A limit larger than the task count prints all tasks.\n  3. `--limit -1` exits with code 2.\n- **Docs:** Add a `python3 todo.py list --limit 2` example to `README.md`.\n- **Check:** `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests`. Under `targeted`, I would run it once before the code change to see the new tests fail, and again after to see them pass.\n- **No commits**, as you asked.\n\n**Evidence:** I read `todo.py`, `tests/test_todo.py` and `README.md`. I have not run any tests. Bash commands outside `git ls-files` were denied in this session, so the test runs above may need you to approve them.\n\n| Next action | Owner | Needs | Authority |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Implement `--limit` using the plan above | Me | Your TDD level (`none` / `targeted` / `strict`), and permission to run `python3 -m unittest` | Implementation is authorized. Commits are not. |"
